--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Third-party data
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

KitePDF is a from-scratch implementation. It contains no third-party source
code. It does contain reference data that a PDF or EPUB implementation has to
carry in order to be correct, listed here with where that data comes from.

1. Adobe Glyph List (AGL)
   Used in: kitepdf-core .../core/font/GlyphList.kt
   Maps PostScript glyph names to Unicode codepoints. Published by Adobe
   Systems Incorporated and redistributable under its own terms.
   Copyright 1997-2002, 2007, 2010 Adobe Systems Incorporated.

2. Adobe CMap resources
   Used in: kitepdf-core .../core/font/PredefinedCMapData.kt
   The predefined CJK CMaps (Adobe-Japan1, Adobe-GB1, Adobe-CNS1, Adobe-Korea1,
   Adobe-KR and their encodings), used to map CID-keyed font codes.
   Copyright Adobe Systems Incorporated, licensed BSD-3-Clause.

3. Metrics for the 14 standard fonts
   Used in: kitepdf-core .../core/font/Standard14Widths.kt
   Glyph widths for Helvetica, Times, Courier, Symbol and ZapfDingbats, as
   published by Adobe in its Core AFM set. A conforming viewer must use these
   values: a PDF naming a standard font carries no widths of its own.
   Metric-compatible substitutes carry the same values by definition.

4. Character encodings defined by ISO 32000-1
   Used in: kitepdf-core .../core/font/Encodings.kt
   StandardEncoding, WinAnsiEncoding, MacRomanEncoding, MacExpertEncoding and
   PDFDocEncoding, as defined in ISO 32000-1, Annex D. The first four tables
   are reproducible from public data: Windows code page 1252 and Mac OS Roman
   as published by the Unicode Consortium, glyph names from the Adobe Glyph
   List above, and the deviations Annex D specifies. MacExpertEncoding is the
   Annex D.4 expert vector, cross-checked against two independent
   Apache-licensed implementations (pdf.js and Apache PDFBox), which agree on
   every code. `EncodingsTest` checks those reproduction rules and the expert
   vector rather than trusting the values.

5. Default rendering rules for HTML
   Used in: kitepdf-epub .../epub/css/UaStylesheet.kt
   The user-agent stylesheet follows the default rendering the specifications
   call for: CSS 2.1, Appendix D and the HTML Standard's Rendering section.

6. Hyphenation patterns from the hyph-utf8 project
   Used in: kitepdf-core .../core/text/hyphen/ (one file per language)
   Knuth-Liang hyphenation pattern data from
   https://github.com/hyphenation/tex-hyphen. Each Kotlin file is a
   mechanical re-wrapping of one upstream pattern file into string chunks;
   the pattern data itself is carried unmodified, and each file's header
   names its own authors, licence and upstream source. The complete
   original files are available at the repository above. German, Spanish,
   French, Italian and Dutch are MIT-licensed; Portuguese is
   BSD-3-Clause; Russian is Copyright 1999-2003 Alexander I. Lebedev,
   LPPL 1.2 or later (https://www.latex-project.org/lppl/lppl-1-2/).

7. URW base 35 fonts
   Used in: kitepdf-core .../core/font/Standard14FontData.kt
   The CFF programs of Nimbus Sans, Nimbus Roman, Nimbus Mono PS, Standard
   Symbols PS and Dingbats, which draw the standard 14 fonts when a PDF does
   not embed them. They are the files that MuPDF ships in resources/fonts/urw,
   stored unmodified as DEFLATE and Base64 by tools/generate_standard14_fonts.py.
   Copyright 2016 by (URW)++ Design & Development, licensed under the SIL Open
   Font License, Version 1.1. The license text is at the top of the file above.

8. PDFium's table for DeviceCMYK
   Used in: kitepdf-core .../core/render/AdobeCmykTable.kt and AdobeCmyk.kt
   The sRGB colours of a 9 by 9 by 9 by 9 grid over C, M, Y and K, and the
   way PDFium steps between grid points, from core/fxge/dib/cfx_cmyk_to_srgb.cpp.
   Copyright 2019 The PDFium Authors, original code copyright 2014 Foxit
   Software Inc., under the BSD-style license of PDFium
   (https://pdfium.googlesource.com/pdfium/+/refs/heads/main/LICENSE).
   tools/generate_adobe_cmyk_table.py extracts the table.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Tools used in development, not distributed
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

MuPDF (mutool) is used as a rendering oracle by the differential test suites.
It is invoked as an external program during testing and no part of it is
included in, linked to, or distributed with KitePDF. Several source comments
name MuPDF where its observable behaviour is the interoperability target that
a KitePDF behaviour was matched against.

PDFium, through the Python binding pypdfium2, is the second rendering oracle
of the parity check. It runs as an external program during testing, and no
part of it is included in, linked to, or distributed with KitePDF.
